                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* \\(c=\\sqrt{T/\\mu }\\) \u2013 wave speed on the string  \n* \\(k=\\omega /c\\) \u2013 wavenumber  \n\nFor a travelling harmonic disturbance we use the complex form  \n\n\\[\ny(x,t)=\\Re \\bigl[\\Phi (x)\\,e^{-i\\omega t}\\bigr],\\qquad \n\\Phi (x)=\\begin{cases}\ne^{ikx}+r\\,e^{-ikx}, & x<0 ,\\\\[2mm]\nt\\,e^{ikx},            & x>0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe incident\u2010wave amplitude is taken as unity.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Boundary conditions at \\(x=0\\)\n\n* **Continuity of displacement**\n\n\\[\ny(0^-,t)=y(0^+,t)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n1+r=t .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n* **Force balance on the point mass**  \n\nThe vertical forces acting on the mass are the net transverse force of the string,\n\\(T(\\partial_x y)_+-T(\\partial_x y)_- \\), and the restoring force of the spring,\n\\(Ky\\).  Newton\u2019s second law gives  \n\n\\[\nT\\Bigl[\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\Bigr]_+-\nT\\Bigl[\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\Bigr]_-\n=\nM\\frac{\\partial ^2 y}{\\partial t^{2}}+Ky .\n\\]\n\nWith the harmonic time factor \\(e^{-i\\omega t}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial ^2 y}{\\partial t^{2}}=-\\omega ^2 y ,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}=ik\\Phi (x).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=0\\) and using (1) :\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT\\bigl[ik\\,t-ik\\,(1-r)\\bigr] &=\n\\bigl(K-M\\omega ^2\\bigr)(1+r) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t=1+r\\),\n\n\\[\n2ikT\\,r=(K-M\\omega ^2)(1+r).\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEquations (1)\u2013(2) are the required relations between \\(r\\) and \\(t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   Solution in terms of \\(\\displaystyle\\eta=\n\\frac{K-M\\omega ^2}{2kT}\\)\n\nFrom (2) we isolate \\(r\\):\n\n\\[\n2ikT\\,r-(K-M\\omega ^2)r=K-M\\omega ^2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nr\\bigl[2ikT-(K-M\\omega ^2)\\bigr]=K-M\\omega ^2 .\n\\]\n\nIntroduce \\(\\eta\\) :\n\n\\[\nK-M\\omega ^2 = 2kT\\,\\eta ,\\qquad\n2ikT-(K-M\\omega ^2)=2kT\\,(i-\\eta ).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,r=\\frac{2kT\\,\\eta}{2kT\\,(i-\\eta)}=\n\\frac{\\eta}{i-\\eta}\\, } .\n\\]\n\nUsing (1),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,t=1+r=1+\\frac{\\eta}{i-\\eta}= \\frac{i}{i-\\eta}\n      =\\frac{1}{1+i\\eta}\\, } .\n\\]\n\nBoth amplitudes are now expressed solely through the single real parameter \\(\\eta\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   Energy coefficients  \n\nFor a monochromatic travelling wave on a stretched string the (time\u2011averaged)\nintensity is  \n\n\\[\nI=\\frac{T}{2}\\,\\omega ^2 |\\text{amplitude}|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nR\\equiv\\frac{I_{\\rm refl}}{I_{\\rm inc}}=|r|^{2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal T\\equiv\\frac{I_{\\rm trans}}{I_{\\rm inc}}=|t|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\eta\\) is real,\n\n\\[\n|i-\\eta|=\\sqrt{1+\\eta ^2},\\qquad\n|r|^{2}= \\frac{\\eta ^2}{1+\\eta ^2},\\qquad\n|t|^{2}= \\frac{1}{1+\\eta ^2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{R=\\frac{\\eta ^2}{1+\\eta ^2}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\mathcal T=\\frac{1}{1+\\eta ^2}},\n\\qquad\nR+\\mathcal T=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus energy is conserved (no dissipation).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Frequency of perfect transmission  \n\nPerfect transmission means \\(R=0\\) (or equivalently \\(|r|=0\\)).  \nFrom the expression for \\(r\\),\n\n\\[\n|r|=0\\iff \\eta =0\\iff K-M\\omega ^2=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the **transparent frequency** is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\omega_{0}= \\sqrt{\\frac{K}{M}} } .\n\\]\n\n**Physical interpretation.**  \nWhen \\(\\omega=\\omega_{0}\\) the inertial reactance of the point mass,\n\\(M\\omega^{2}\\), exactly balances the static stiffness of the spring,\n\\(K\\).  The combined \u201cdynamic impedance\u2019\u2019 of the mass\u2011spring system then\nequals the characteristic impedance of the string,\n\\(Z_{0}=T/c\\).  The junction therefore presents no discontinuity to the\nincoming wave; the string sees a continuation of itself and the wave\npasses through without being reflected.  This is the familiar phenomenon\nof *impedance matching* (or *resonance transparency*) in loss\u2011free\nmechanical systems.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer four parts.\n\nFirst, set up continuity conditions at x=0: displacement y continuous; slope? For string with point mass attached via spring, we have force balance on mass: T * (\u2202y/\u2202x)_right - T*(\u2202y/\u2202x)_left = M*y'' + K*y (since spring attached to fixed support). Actually spring exerts restoring force K*y (assuming equilibrium at zero). So equation: T(\u2202y/\u2202x)_+ - T(\u2202y/\u2202x)_- = M \u2202^2 y/\u2202t^2 + K y evaluated at x=0. Use harmonic time dependence e^{-i \u03c9 t}. Then \u2202/\u2202t -> -i \u03c9. So M(-i \u03c9)^2 y = -M \u03c9^2 y. So RHS = -M \u03c9^2 y + K y = (K - M \u03c9^2) y.\n\nNow write incident wave A_i = 1 (choose amplitude 1), reflected r, transmitted t. At x=0-, displacement = (1 + r) e^{i k 0}=1+r. At x=0+, displacement = t e^{i k 0}=t. Continuity gives 1 + r = t.\n\nSlope: derivative w.r.t x: For x<0, y' = i k (1 + r e^{-2 i k x? Wait derivative of e^{-ikx}: -i k r e^{-ikx}. At x=0, derivative = i k (1 - r). Because derivative of e^{ikx} is i k e^{ikx}, derivative of r e^{-ikx} is -i k r e^{-ikx}. Evaluate at 0: i k (1 - r). For x>0, derivative = i k t e^{ikx} => i k t at x=0.\n\nThus slope condition: T[i k t - i k (1 - r)] = (K - M \u03c9^2)(1 + r) . Actually using earlier expression: T(\u2202y/\u2202x)_+ - T(\u2202y/\u2202x)_- = (K - M \u03c9^2) y. So plug:\n\nT[i k t] - T[i k (1 - r)] = (K - M \u03c9^2)(1 + r).\n\nSimplify: i k T [t - (1 - r)] = (K - M \u03c9^2)(1 + r).\n\nBut note i k T = i (\u03c9/c) T = i \u03c9 \u03bc (since c = sqrt(T/\u03bc) => T = \u03bc c^2). Indeed T = \u03bc c^2, so i k T = i (\u03c9/c) \u03bc c^2 = i \u03c9 \u03bc c = i \u03c9 \u03bc c? Wait compute: k = \u03c9 / c, so k T = (\u03c9/c) T = (\u03c9/c) \u03bc c^2 = \u03c9 \u03bc c. So i k T = i \u03c9 \u03bc c. But maybe keep symbolic.\n\nEquation (1): continuity: t = 1 + r.\n\nEquation (2): i k T [t - (1 - r)] = (K - M \u03c9^2)(1 + r).\n\nPlug t = 1 + r: then t - (1 - r) = (1 + r) - (1 - r) = 2 r. So left side becomes i k T * 2 r = 2 i k T r.\n\nRight side: (K - M \u03c9^2)(1 + r).\n\nThus 2 i k T r = (K - M \u03c9^2)(1 + r).\n\nSolve for r: bring terms: 2 i k T r - (K - M \u03c9^2) r = (K - M \u03c9^2). Factor r: r[2 i k T - (K - M \u03c9^2)] = (K - M \u03c9^2). Hence\n\nr = (K - M \u03c9^2) / [2 i k T - (K - M \u03c9^2)].\n\nThen t = 1 + r = 1 + above.\n\nDefine \u03b7 = (K - M \u03c9^2)/(2 k T). Note they defined \u03b7 = (K - M \u03c9^2)/(2 k T). Good. Then rewrite denominator: 2 i k T - (K - M \u03c9^2) = 2 i k T - (K - M \u03c9^2) = 2 i k T - (K - M \u03c9^2). Write numerator N = K - M \u03c9^2. Denominator D = 2 i k T - N.\n\nFactor 2 k T: D = 2 k T (i - N/(2 k T)) = 2 k T (i - \u03b7). Since \u03b7 = N/(2 k T). So D = 2 k T (i - \u03b7). Numerator N = 2 k T \u03b7.\n\nThus r = (2 k T \u03b7) / [2 k T (i - \u03b7)] = \u03b7 / (i - \u03b7). Multiply numerator and denominator conj? Keep.\n\nSo r = \u03b7 / (i - \u03b7). Equivalent to r = \u03b7 (i+\u03b7)/ (1+\u03b7^2)? Let's compute: 1/(i-\u03b7) multiply top/bottom by (-i-\u03b7): (i-\u03b7)(-i-\u03b7) = -i^2 - i \u03b7 + i \u03b7 + \u03b7^2 = 1 + \u03b7^2. Actually (i-\u03b7)(-i-\u03b7) = -(i)(i) - i \u03b7 + i \u03b7 - \u03b7^2? Let's do systematically: (i-\u03b7)(-i-\u03b7) = i*(-i) + i*(-\u03b7) - \u03b7*(-i) - \u03b7*\u03b7 = -i^2 - i \u03b7 + i \u03b7 - \u03b7^2 = 1 - \u03b7^2? Wait -i^2 = 1. Then - i \u03b7 + i \u03b7 cancel. So result = 1 - \u03b7^2. Hmm not 1+\u03b7^2. Let's check alternative: multiply by (i+\u03b7): (i-\u03b7)(i+\u03b7) = i^2 - \u03b7^2 = -1 - \u03b7^2. Not good. Let's just leave as \u03b7/(i-\u03b7). Could also express as r = \u03b7 (i+\u03b7)/ (1+\u03b7^2)?? Let's test: Suppose we multiply numerator and denominator by (i+\u03b7): r = \u03b7 (i+\u03b7) / [(i-\u03b7)(i+\u03b7)] = \u03b7 (i+\u03b7) / (i^2 - \u03b7^2) = \u03b7 (i+\u03b7) / (-1 - \u03b7^2) = - \u03b7 (i+\u03b7) / (1+\u03b7^2). That's different sign. Let's compute directly: (i-\u03b7)(i+\u03b7) = i^2 - \u03b7^2 = -1 - \u03b7^2. So denominator = -(1+\u03b7^2). So r = \u03b7 (i+\u03b7)/ (-(1+\u03b7^2)) = - \u03b7 (i+\u03b7)/(1+\u03b7^2). That seems plausible but earlier we had denominator 2kT(i-\u03b7). So r = \u03b7/(i-\u03b7). That's fine.\n\nTransmission amplitude t = 1 + r = 1 + \u03b7/(i-\u03b7) = (i-\u03b7 + \u03b7)/ (i-\u03b7) = i/(i-\u03b7). So t = i/(i-\u03b7). Or equivalently t = 1/(1 - \u03b7/i) = 1/(1 + i \u03b7?). Let's compute: i/(i-\u03b7) = divide numerator and denominator by i: 1/(1 - \u03b7/i) = 1/(1 + i \u03b7). Since \u03b7/i = -i \u03b7, so 1 - \u03b7/i = 1 + i \u03b7. Yes t = 1/(1 + i \u03b7). Good.\n\nNow part (2) done.\n\nPart (3): Energy reflection coefficient R = |r|^2 (for unit incident intensity). Transmission coefficient for energy across interface: For traveling waves on string, power proportional to (T/2) \u03c9^2 |amplitude|^2? Actually intensity I = (T/2) \u03c9^2 A^2 (for sinusoidal wave). For incident wave amplitude 1, reflected amplitude r, transmitted amplitude t. Power incident = (T/2) \u03c9^2 |1|^2. Reflected = (T/2) \u03c9^2 |r|^2. Transmitted = (T/2) \u03c9^2 |t|^2 times factor due to change in wave speed? On right side, wave speed same c because tension and density unchanged? Actually after point mass, string continues same properties, so speed same. So transmission coefficient = |t|^2. However there may be factor due to different direction? No.\n\nBut careful: The transmitted wave travels to the right, its intensity is (T/2) \u03c9^2 |t|^2. So ratio = |t|^2.\n\nCheck energy conservation: R + T = |r|^2 + |t|^2 = 1? Let's verify using expressions.\n\nCompute |r|^2 = |\u03b7|^2 / |i-\u03b7|^2. Compute |i-\u03b7|^2 = (Re(i-\u03b7))^2+(Im(i-\u03b7))^2 = ( -Im \u03b7 )^2 + (1 - Re \u03b7)^2? Actually \u03b7 could be real? In our definition \u03b7 = (K - M \u03c9^2)/(2 k T). This is real because all parameters real. So \u03b7 is real. Then i-\u03b7 has magnitude sqrt(1 + \u03b7^2). So |i-\u03b7| = sqrt(1+\u03b7^2). Thus |r|^2 = \u03b7^2 / (1+\u03b7^2). Similarly |t|^2 = |i/(i-\u03b7)|^2 = 1/(1+\u03b7^2). Sum = (\u03b7^2 + 1)/(1+\u03b7^2) = 1. Good.\n\nThus R = \u03b7^2/(1+\u03b7^2); T = 1/(1+\u03b7^2). Provide explanation.\n\nPart (4): Perfect transmission when R=0 => \u03b7=0 => K - M \u03c9^2 = 0 => \u03c9 = sqrt(K/M). That's the natural frequency of the mass-spring system (undamped). At resonance, the impedance matches, leading to no reflection. Physical reason: When the load's mechanical impedance equals characteristic impedance of string, the boundary acts like continuation of string; energy passes without reflection. At \u03c9 such that effective mass term cancels spring stiffness, the point mass behaves as if it were part of string.\n\nAlternatively, perfect transmission also when \u03b7\u2192\u221e? That would give R\u22481, not perfect transmission. So only \u03b7=0 yields zero reflection.\n\nExplain: At \u03c9 = sqrt(K/M), the dynamic compliance of the mass-spring equals that of the string, making the junction transparent.\n\nProvide final answer.\n\nLet's craft final response with steps."
